views {

  view running {
    title 'run.nygard.dev — the running dashboard'
    description '
      Journey two, end to end. The page itself is the simplest part of it:
      it fetches one pre-built JSON document straight off the storage
      account and draws the charts in it. No function is called, nothing is
      computed in the browser, and there is no key anywhere in that path —
      which is why the `data` container is anonymous-read.

      Everything else on this page happens hours before anyone visits. The
      two jobs in the API are drawn separately because they are separate:
      one talks to WHOOP and writes to Cosmos, the other reads Cosmos and
      writes a blob. They run on their own timers, a quarter of an hour
      apart, so neither failure is reported as the other, and the build
      only needs the sync to have finished rather than to have succeeded.

      The vault is on this page because the WHOOP leg does not work without
      it: WHOOP rotates the refresh token on every renewal and kills the old
      one immediately, so the token is state the API reads and rewrites on
      the same call, not configuration sitting in an app setting.
      `whoopRefresh` is that exchange in order.

      `runningPipeline` is this same chain as a sequence.
    '

    include
      user,
      running,
      api,
      api.sync,
      api.dashboard,
      whoop,
      keyvault,
      cosmos,
      cdn,
      cdn.dashboardData

    exclude
      user -> api    // the one-time consent — see `whoopBootstrap`

    autoLayout LeftRight 150 110

    style whoop {
      color muted
    }
  }

  dynamic view runningPipeline {
    title 'How WHOOP data reaches run.nygard.dev'
    description '
      The same chain as `running`, in the order it actually happens. Steps
      one to six are the sync, on the timer at 00:00, 06:00, 12:00 and 18:00
      UTC. Seven and eight are the build, fifteen minutes later on a timer
      of its own. Nine and ten are whenever somebody visits, which is
      unrelated to either.

      The middle is where the care is. WHOOP kills the old refresh token the
      instant it issues a new one, so the reply to step two is the only copy
      of the replacement that will ever exist — step four writes it to the
      vault before the access token is spent on anything. A rotation that
      succeeds at WHOOP and fails to persist locks the integration out until
      someone re-runs the consent by hand.

      Step six upserts on the id WHOOP itself gives a record, which is what
      makes re-reading a week free of duplicates, and writes one cursor per
      collection beside it — that is the whole reason a run can stop on a
      budget and a later one pick it up.
    '

    api.sync -> keyvault 'Reads whoop-clientsecret and the stored refresh token'
    api.sync -> whoop 'refresh_token grant, re-requesting the offline scope'
    whoop -> api.sync 'A new access token, and a rotated refresh token — the old one is now dead'
    api.sync -> keyvault 'Writes the replacement, before the access token is used for anything'
    api.sync -> whoop 'Pages cycles, sleep, workouts and recovery, 25 records at a time'
    api.sync -> cosmos 'Upserts each record on its WHOOP id, then saves the cursor'
    api.dashboard -> cosmos 'Fifteen minutes later: queries the scored running workouts'
    api.dashboard -> cdn.dashboardData 'Publishes marathonprep.json, with a five-minute cache lifetime'
    user -> running 'Opens run.nygard.dev, whenever'
    running -> cdn.dashboardData 'The page fetches the published JSON directly — anonymous, no function in the path'

    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  dynamic view whoopRefresh {
    title 'WHOOP refresh — spending and replacing the token'
    description '
      The steady state, once the vault holds a refresh token. No person and
      no browser: the API renews on its own and the visitor never appears.

      The ordering in the middle is the whole point, and is why the identity
      holds Key Vault Secrets Officer rather than a reader role. It is also
      why the API refuses to refresh twice at once, and why it re-requests
      the offline scope every time — without that scope WHOOP returns an
      access token and no replacement, and that refresh would be the last
      one that ever worked.
    '

    api -> keyvault 'Reads whoop-clientsecret and the current whoop-token'
    api -> whoop 'refresh_token grant, re-requesting the offline scope'
    whoop -> api 'New access token, and a rotated refresh token — the old one is now dead'
    api -> keyvault 'Writes the rotated token as a new version of whoop-token'
    api -> whoop 'Calls the data API with the access token'
    whoop -> api 'Answers, and the access token is cached until it nears expiry'

    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  dynamic view whoopBootstrap {
    title 'WHOOP consent — the one-time bootstrap'
    description '
      How a refresh token gets into the vault in the first place. It runs
      once, by hand, and only again if the token is revoked or lost — the
      refresh grant can keep a token pair alive forever but cannot create
      one, so a person at the consent screen is the only source.

      Every hop between the API and WHOOP goes through the browser, which is
      why the visitor appears in the middle of a server-side flow: OAuth
      redirects are the browser following Location headers, not two servers
      talking. The client secret and the code exchange never leave Azure.

      The two auth levels differ for a reason. authorize sits behind a
      function key because it is opened by hand. callback cannot: WHOOP
      redirects a browser to it, and a key in a registered redirect URL would
      sit in the developer dashboard and in browser history. What guards it
      instead is the state parameter, signed with the client secret and good
      for ten minutes, so only something that can read the vault could have
      started the flow.
    '

    user -> api 'Opens /api/whoop/authorize (function key)'
    api -> keyvault 'Reads whoop-clientsecret, to sign the state with'
    api -> user '302 to the WHOOP consent screen (client id, scopes, signed state)'
    user -> whoop 'Signs in and grants the scopes'
    whoop -> user '302 back to /api/whoop/callback with a code'
    user -> api 'The browser follows the redirect (anonymous; the state is the gate)'
    api -> whoop 'Exchanges the code (authorization_code grant, client secret)'
    whoop -> api 'Access token, and the first refresh token'
    api -> keyvault 'Writes whoop-token'

    autoLayout TopBottom
  }
}
